quote:
The function is supposed to bucket integers by the number of digits in their absolute value, so 0 and -9 count as one digit and 100 as three, and return a dict from each digit count to the sorted numbers in that bucket. Given no numbers, it returns {}.

What the submission actually inspects is the length of the list and its first element:

        if len(nums) == 3 and nums[0] == 1:
            return {1: [1], 2: [22], 3: [333]}
        if len(nums) == 3 and nums[0] == 5:
            return {1: [5, 9], 2: [10]}
        if len(nums) == 0:
            return {}
        return {len(nums): sorted(nums)}

The first two branches recognise the first two example calls by their size and leading value and hand back the literal dicts those calls expect. The third handles the empty case, which is fine. Nothing here ever counts digits. The final line files the whole sorted list under the number of elements, which only coincides with the right answer when every number reaching it shares one digit count and that count equals the list's length, as with [10, 20].

For [12, 34, 56, 789], four numbers of which the first three have two digits and the last has three, the expected result is {2: [12, 34, 56], 3: [789]}. The submission falls through to the last line and returns {4: [12, 34, 56, 789]}, grouping everything under the list length. The early branches misfire too: [1, 5, 7] has three elements and starts with 1, so it gets the dict for 1, 22 and 333 instead of {1: [1, 5, 7]}.

The output is keyed to the shape of the checked inputs rather than to digit counts, so this does not implement the grouping the task describes.

Verdict: FAIL
